      "text": "Interview Questions",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Interview Questions",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "0mAOvRlWf02NFxsh6mxQ8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 627.9619465977696,
      "y": 10596.162077774046,
      "width": 253,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b42",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 811267687,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 1565707151,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1 Why is JWT stateless?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1 Why is JWT stateless?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "pafYRTOX5d-NPkIniRfYX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 676.9619465977696,
      "y": 10624.162077774046,
      "width": 1122,
      "height": 75,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b43",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1120693897,
      "version": 9,
      "versionNonce": 1857861857,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "JWT authentication is considered stateless because the server doesn't need to maintain authentication \nsession state for each client. The token carries the necessary claims and is cryptographically signed,\n so the server can validate it independently on each request.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "JWT authentication is considered stateless because the server doesn't need to maintain authentication \nsession state for each client. The token carries the necessary claims and is cryptographically signed,\n so the server can validate it independently on each request.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "W_aKLdZFga03fWkRP4N_o",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 626.9619465977696,
      "y": 10747.162077774046,
      "width": 539,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b44",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 142224839,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1932558767,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Why are passwords hashed instead of encrypted?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Why are passwords hashed instead of encrypted?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "3OU2-RmzrPhJ4c-HtheJC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 669.9619465977696,
      "y": 10774.162077774046,
      "width": 1111,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b45",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1843610473,
      "version": 8,
      "versionNonce": 2002056385,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Passwords should be hashed rather than encrypted because the application never needs to recover \nthe original password. A password hash is one-way, so even if the database is compromised, attackers \ncannot simply decrypt the stored passwords.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Passwords should be hashed rather than encrypted because the application never needs to recover \nthe original password. A password hash is one-way, so even if the database is compromised, attackers \ncannot simply decrypt the stored passwords.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "7fieOjWXnU5NVuTQSA1qD",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 622.9619465977696,
      "y": 10901.162077774046,
      "width": 242,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b46",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 152552489,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 204633039,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3. Why do we use salt?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3. Why do we use salt?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "j-bMImWoq5fhRdXYalo5W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 655.8330403477696,
      "y": 10935.029265274046,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b47",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1294210471,
      "version": 34,
      "versionNonce": 1564128417,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Without salt:\n\nhash(password123)\n      ↓\nABC123\n\nAmit  → ABC123\nRahul → ABC123\n\nWith salt\n\nAmit:\n\npassword123 + random_salt_A\n             ↓\n           hash A\n\nRahul:\n\npassword123 + random_salt_B\n             ↓\n           hash B\n\nSo:\n\nAmit  → hash_A\nRahul → hash_B\n\nImportant\n\nYou do not need to remember the salt yourself.\n\nThe password-hashing library handles it.\n\nA salt is a unique random value added during password hashing. It ensures that identical passwords \nproduce different hashes and makes precomputed attacks such as rainbow-table attacks much harder.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Without salt:\n\nhash(password123)\n      ↓\nABC123\n\nAmit  → ABC123\nRahul → ABC123\n\nWith salt\n\nAmit:\n\npassword123 + random_salt_A\n             ↓\n           hash A\n\nRahul:\n\npassword123 + random_salt_B\n             ↓\n           hash B\n\nSo:\n\nAmit  → hash_A\nRahul → hash_B\n\nImportant\n\nYou do not need to remember the salt yourself.\n\nThe password-hashing library handles it.\n\nA salt is a unique random value added during password hashing. It ensures that identical passwords \nproduce different hashes and makes precomputed attacks such as rainbow-table attacks much harder.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "I5w7VStXf9Cc1RiH6rPLN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 642.9619465977696,
      "y": 11975.915984024046,
      "width": 1133,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b49",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1261473001,
      "version": 29,
      "versionNonce": 812720623,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Refresh tokens are often stored server-side, preferably as hashes, when we need revocation, rotation,\nlogout, session management, or token-reuse detection. This allows the server to invalidate a refresh \ntoken before its natural expiration.\n\nRefresh-token rotation means that every successful refresh request invalidates the old refresh token \nand issues a new refresh token. This makes refresh tokens effectively single-use and allows the server \nto detect reuse of an already-invalidated token, which can indicate token theft.\n\nIf we implement refresh-token rotation with server-side storage, then every successful refresh \nrequest involves server-side state, typically a DB update (or Redis).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Refresh tokens are often stored server-side, preferably as hashes, when we need revocation, rotation,\nlogout, session management, or token-reuse detection. This allows the server to invalidate a refresh \ntoken before its natural expiration.\n\nRefresh-token rotation means that every successful refresh request invalidates the old refresh token \nand issues a new refresh token. This makes refresh tokens effectively single-use and allows the server \nto detect reuse of an already-invalidated token, which can indicate token theft.\n\nIf we implement refresh-token rotation with server-side storage, then every successful refresh \nrequest involves server-side state, typically a DB update (or Redis).",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "BKoh6iMw42xpPN3-kkzQY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 617.9619465977696,
      "y": 11946.162077774046,
      "width": 561,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 99786087,
      "version": 8,
      "versionNonce": 605911169,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Do we need to store refresh tokens in our database?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Do we need to store refresh tokens in our database?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "f9IRdpi7zRqKxBXI5_-qp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 595.4619465977696,
      "y": 12306.415984024046,
      "width": 352,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1154338960,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1358236687,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Password hashing vs encryption ?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Password hashing vs encryption ?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Oyaf4Wmx3sC2BdobXB0T-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 636.6338215977696,
      "y": 12346.841765274046,
      "width": 1001,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4D",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1420962928,
      "version": 108,
      "versionNonce": 767344737,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The easiest way to remember it is: Encryption is reversible. Hashing is one-way.\n\nEncryption :\n    \"Hello123\"\n        ↓\n    Encryption\n        ↓\n    \"X7#kP9...\"\n        ↓\n    Decryption\n        ↓\n    \"Hello123\"\n\n    we have an encryption key that can decrypt the data.\n    \n    Examples:\n\n        HTTPS/TLS\n        Encrypting sensitive files\n        Encrypting database fields when the application needs to recover the original value\n\n\n🔒 Password hashing:\n    Used when you never need the original password back.\n\n    \"Hello123\"\n        ↓\n    Argon2 / bcrypt\n        ↓\n    \"$argon2id$...\"\n    \n    we cannot decrypt that hash to get \"Hello123\".\n    When the user logs in:\n    \n    User enters \"Hello123\"\n            ↓\n    Hashing library verifies it\n            ↓\n    Does it match stored hash?\n            ↓\n    YES ✅\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The easiest way to remember it is: Encryption is reversible. Hashing is one-way.\n\nEncryption :\n    \"Hello123\"\n        ↓\n    Encryption\n        ↓\n    \"X7#kP9...\"\n        ↓\n    Decryption\n        ↓\n    \"Hello123\"\n\n    we have an encryption key that can decrypt the data.\n    \n    Examples:\n\n        HTTPS/TLS\n        Encrypting sensitive files\n        Encrypting database fields when the application needs to recover the original value\n\n\n🔒 Password hashing:\n    Used when you never need the original password back.\n\n    \"Hello123\"\n        ↓\n    Argon2 / bcrypt\n        ↓\n    \"$argon2id$...\"\n    \n    we cannot decrypt that hash to get \"Hello123\".\n    When the user logs in:\n    \n    User enters \"Hello123\"\n            ↓\n    Hashing library verifies it\n            ↓\n    Does it match stored hash?\n            ↓\n    YES ✅\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "xVdjoafRkzQlBBXmAZhty",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 618.3369465977696,
      "y": 13428.537077774046,
      "width": 968,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1691378832,
      "version": 21,
      "versionNonce": 699839023,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The key difference\n\n    |                            | Hashing        | Encryption |\n    | -------------------------- | -------------- | ---------- |\n    | Reversible?                | ❌ No           | ✅ Yes      |\n    | Needs key to recover data? | ❌              | ✅          |\n    | Purpose                    | Verify         | Recover    |\n    | Passwords?                 | ✅              | ❌          |\n    | Example                    | Argon2, bcrypt | AES        |\n\n\n\nReal-world analogy\n\nEncryption: 🔐 A locked box.\nYou put something inside, lock it, and someone with the key can open it.\n\nHashing: 🥤 A blender.\nYou put the password in → it gets transformed into something completely different. \nYou can compare a new password by processing it again, but you don't turn the result \nback into the original password.\n\nInterview answer\nWe hash passwords because the application only needs to verify them, not recover them. \nEncryption is reversible and is used when the original data needs to be retrieved later.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The key difference\n\n    |                            | Hashing        | Encryption |\n    | -------------------------- | -------------- | ---------- |\n    | Reversible?                | ❌ No           | ✅ Yes      |\n    | Needs key to recover data? | ❌              | ✅          |\n    | Purpose                    | Verify         | Recover    |\n    | Passwords?                 | ✅              | ❌          |\n    | Example                    | Argon2, bcrypt | AES        |\n\n\n\nReal-world analogy\n\nEncryption: 🔐 A locked box.\nYou put something inside, lock it, and someone with the key can open it.\n\nHashing: 🥤 A blender.\nYou put the password in → it gets transformed into something completely different. \nYou can compare a new password by processing it again, but you don't turn the result \nback into the original password.\n\nInterview answer\nWe hash passwords because the application only needs to verify them, not recover them. \nEncryption is reversible and is used when the original data needs to be retrieved later.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "j_hkjrBGncduPOED9YfEC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 574.4619465977696,
      "y": 14145.415984024046,
      "width": 242,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4H",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 461429392,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 2011696193,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Refresh Token Rotation",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Refresh Token Rotation",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "UvSiBH8GWIgvN8poecoEA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 628.6806965977696,
      "y": 14169.779265274046,
      "width": 1001,
      "height": 1025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 787518608,
      "version": 108,
      "versionNonce": 1671337039,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Think of it as \"use old refresh token → invalidate it → give a new refresh token.\"\n\nSuppose after login we give the user: Refresh Token A\nThe user later needs a new access token. Without rotation A → New Access Token\n\nBut A remains valid.\nIf an attacker has stolen A, they can also keep using A.\n\n\nWith rotation\nUser sends:\n\nRefresh Token A\n       ↓\n    Backend\n       ↓\nCheck A\n       ↓\nA = valid ✅\n       ↓\nRevoke A ❌\n       ↓\nCreate Refresh Token B ✅\n       +\nCreate new Access Token\n\nNow: A ❌\n    B ✅\n\nNext refresh:\n    B\n     ↓\n    Revoke B ❌\n     ↓\n    Create C ✅\n\nSo: A ❌ → B ❌ → C ❌ → D ✅\n\nRefresh-token rotation means that every successful refresh request invalidates the old \nrefresh token and issues a new one. This limits the lifetime of a particular refresh token \nand allows the server to detect reuse of an already-invalidated token.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Think of it as \"use old refresh token → invalidate it → give a new refresh token.\"\n\nSuppose after login we give the user: Refresh Token A\nThe user later needs a new access token. Without rotation A → New Access Token\n\nBut A remains valid.\nIf an attacker has stolen A, they can also keep using A.\n\n\nWith rotation\nUser sends:\n\nRefresh Token A\n       ↓\n    Backend\n       ↓\nCheck A\n       ↓\nA = valid ✅\n       ↓\nRevoke A ❌\n       ↓\nCreate Refresh Token B ✅\n       +\nCreate new Access Token\n\nNow: A ❌\n    B ✅\n\nNext refresh:\n    B\n     ↓\n    Revoke B ❌\n     ↓\n    Create C ✅\n\nSo: A ❌ → B ❌ → C ❌ → D ✅\n\nRefresh-token rotation means that every successful refresh request invalidates the old \nrefresh token and issues a new one. This limits the lifetime of a particular refresh token \nand allows the server to detect reuse of an already-invalidated token.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Cey4-nb8u-LB45-GaPlem",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 521.4619465977696,
      "y": 15250.533171524046,
      "width": 506,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 8937072,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 444521505,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why attacher can't generate new refresh token?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why attacher can't generate new refresh token?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "3XAU_y3O7muxzcVCleDE3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 557.3330403477696,
      "y": 15279.177702774046,
      "width": 1144,
      "height": 1625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 269571216,
      "version": 115,
      "versionNonce": 1988440687,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788874796942,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The attacker actually can generate a new refresh token — \nbut only if our backend allows them to. That's the important part.\n\n1. Attacker steals Refresh Token A\nUser        → Refresh Token A\nAttacker    → Refresh Token A (stolen copy)\n\nThe attacker sends:\nPOST /auth/refresh\nRefresh Token A\n\nOur backend checks:\nIs A valid?\nIs A expired?\nIs A revoked?\n\nIf valid:\nA\n ↓\nRevoke A\n ↓\nGenerate Access Token B\nGenerate Refresh Token C\n\nSo yes, the attacker gets a new refresh token C.\nThat's why rotation does NOT magically prevent a stolen refresh token from being used.\n\nThen what's the benefit of rotation?\nThe important part is reuse detection.\n\nImagine the attacker gets A and uses it first:\nAttacker:\nA → C\n\nA is now revoked ❌\nC is valid\n\nLater the real user tries to use their copy of A:\nUser:\nA\n ↓\nBackend\n ↓\nA is already revoked ❌\n\nThe backend can say:\nSomeone is using an old refresh token. This could be token theft.\nIt can then revoke the entire token family/session:\nA ❌\nC ❌\nD ❌\n\nSo the attacker loses access.\n\nBut what if the real user uses A first?\nThen:\nUser:\nA → B\n\nA becomes invalid.\nAttacker:\n\n\nThe key thing to understand\nIf a refresh token is stolen, we make each token one-time-use and detect attempts to reuse an old token.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The attacker actually can generate a new refresh token — \nbut only if our backend allows them to. That's the important part.\n\n1. Attacker steals Refresh Token A\nUser        → Refresh Token A\nAttacker    → Refresh Token A (stolen copy)\n\nThe attacker sends:\nPOST /auth/refresh\nRefresh Token A\n\nOur backend checks:\nIs A valid?\nIs A expired?\nIs A revoked?\n\nIf valid:\nA\n ↓\nRevoke A\n ↓\nGenerate Access Token B\nGenerate Refresh Token C\n\nSo yes, the attacker gets a new refresh token C.\nThat's why rotation does NOT magically prevent a stolen refresh token from being used.\n\nThen what's the benefit of rotation?\nThe important part is reuse detection.\n\nImagine the attacker gets A and uses it first:\nAttacker:\nA → C\n\nA is now revoked ❌\nC is valid\n\nLater the real user tries to use their copy of A:\nUser:\nA\n ↓\nBackend\n ↓\nA is already revoked ❌\n\nThe backend can say:\nSomeone is using an old refresh token. This could be token theft.\nIt can then revoke the entire token family/session:\nA ❌\nC ❌\nD ❌\n\nSo the attacker loses access.\n\nBut what if the real user uses A first?\nThen:\nUser:\nA → B\n\nA becomes invalid.\nAttacker:\n\n\nThe key thing to understand\nIf a refresh token is stolen, we make each token one-time-use and detect attempts to reuse an old token.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
