    },
    {
      "id": "icy-WUzj6Jyd2JXDDMLYK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 419.5830403477696,
      "y": 21587.294890274046,
      "width": 385,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4e",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1497251983,
      "version": 32,
      "versionNonce": 1853008289,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788875610886,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🎯 Today's first exercise\n\nQuestion 1 : Suppose we have:\nUser\nOrganization\nOrganizationMember\n\nAnd Rahul belongs to:\nGoogle → Developer\nTechNova → Consultant\n\nWhere should we store Rahul's role?\n\nA) User.role\nB) Organization.role\nC) OrganizationMember.role\nD) Somewhere else",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🎯 Today's first exercise\n\nQuestion 1 : Suppose we have:\nUser\nOrganization\nOrganizationMember\n\nAnd Rahul belongs to:\nGoogle → Developer\nTechNova → Consultant\n\nWhere should we store Rahul's role?\n\nA) User.role\nB) Organization.role\nC) OrganizationMember.role\nD) Somewhere else",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uLzBDDZlyCA3EK-I5IFcG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 453.4541340977696,
      "y": 22029.290984024046,
      "width": 836,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4f",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 745131311,
      "version": 53,
      "versionNonce": 173330959,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788875761961,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Correct. ✅\nYou chose C — OrganizationMember.role.\nYour reasoning is exactly right:\nA user's role can be different in different organizations.\n\nFor example:\nUser\nRahul\n\nMemberships:\nOrganizationMember\n--------------------------------\nRahul → Google    → Developer\nRahul → TechNova → Consultant\n\nSo role belongs to the relationship between Rahul and the organization, \nnot to Rahul himself.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong\nA) User.role ❌ -> Would mean Rahul has only one role:\nB) Organization.role ❌ ->  Would mean everyone in Google has the same role:\n\nThe general rule 🔑\n\nWhenever you see a statement like:\n\"X has a different value depending on Y\"\nthat value often belongs to the relationship/junction table.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Correct. ✅\nYou chose C — OrganizationMember.role.\nYour reasoning is exactly right:\nA user's role can be different in different organizations.\n\nFor example:\nUser\nRahul\n\nMemberships:\nOrganizationMember\n--------------------------------\nRahul → Google    → Developer\nRahul → TechNova → Consultant\n\nSo role belongs to the relationship between Rahul and the organization, \nnot to Rahul himself.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong\nA) User.role ❌ -> Would mean Rahul has only one role:\nB) Organization.role ❌ ->  Would mean everyone in Google has the same role:\n\nThe general rule 🔑\n\nWhenever you see a statement like:\n\"X has a different value depending on Y\"\nthat value often belongs to the relationship/junction table.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "II2KtyQgHEx66e5KZknv7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 369.4619465977696,
      "y": 22784.415984024046,
      "width": 1188,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4g",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 577943663,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 973716175,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788875895904,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 2: Why should we create a Custom User model in Django instead of using Django's default User model?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 2: Why should we create a Custom User model in Django instead of using Django's default User model?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "PfX52Lw4ShTWvvBLL3ZWw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 434.0439778477696,
      "y": 22824.685515274046,
      "width": 1012,
      "height": 1250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2067879233,
      "version": 51,
      "versionNonce": 476938945,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788876061762,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Django's default User model gives us common fields such as:\nusername\npassword\nemail\nfirst_name\nlast_name\n...\n\nBut our application may have requirements such as:\nUser\n├── email\n├── password\n├── first_name\n├── last_name\n├── phone_number\n├── email_verified\n├── profile_image\n├── created_at\n└── updated_at\n\nMore importantly, we may want email to be the login identity instead of username.\n\nFor example:\nPOST /login\n\nemail: amit@gmail.com\npassword: ********\n\nrather than:\nusername: amit123\npassword: ********\nWith a custom user model, we control these decisions from the beginning.\n\n\nWhy create it from Day 1?\nThis is important.\nChanging from Django's default User to a custom user model after your project already has \nmany migrations and relationships can be painful.\n\n\nWe use a custom User model when the application's authentication requirements differ from \nDjango's default User model. It gives us control over fields, authentication identity, and \nfuture extensibility. We should generally define it at the beginning of the project because \nchanging the user model later can be difficult due to existing relationships and migrations.\n\n\nYour core reasoning was correct. The missing points were:\nWe control the authentication identity (e.g., email instead of username).\nCustomization isn't only about adding fields.\nCreating it early avoids painful migration/relationship changes later.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Django's default User model gives us common fields such as:\nusername\npassword\nemail\nfirst_name\nlast_name\n...\n\nBut our application may have requirements such as:\nUser\n├── email\n├── password\n├── first_name\n├── last_name\n├── phone_number\n├── email_verified\n├── profile_image\n├── created_at\n└── updated_at\n\nMore importantly, we may want email to be the login identity instead of username.\n\nFor example:\nPOST /login\n\nemail: amit@gmail.com\npassword: ********\n\nrather than:\nusername: amit123\npassword: ********\nWith a custom user model, we control these decisions from the beginning.\n\n\nWhy create it from Day 1?\nThis is important.\nChanging from Django's default User to a custom user model after your project already has \nmany migrations and relationships can be painful.\n\n\nWe use a custom User model when the application's authentication requirements differ from \nDjango's default User model. It gives us control over fields, authentication identity, and \nfuture extensibility. We should generally define it at the beginning of the project because \nchanging the user model later can be difficult due to existing relationships and migrations.\n\n\nYour core reasoning was correct. The missing points were:\nWe control the authentication identity (e.g., email instead of username).\nCustomization isn't only about adding fields.\nCreating it early avoids painful migration/relationship changes later.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    }
  ],
